# Sources for the oil committee and proposal dispute: Painter and Brew,
# The Struggle for Iran, ch. 1; official Special Oil Commission minutes,
# first period. The recurring preparation and its effects are counterfactual.
title: Oil Committee Preparation
new-page: true
is-card: true
tags: parliamentary_affairs
view-if: oil_committee_preparation_timer <= 0 and majles_convened = 1 and nationalization_approved_majles = 0
on-arrival: month_actions += 1; oil_committee_preparation_timer = 5

= Oil Committee Preparation

The oil dispute contains several separate questions: ownership, operations,
marketing, compensation, finance, personnel authority, and review. Committee
work can clarify those terms, build a majority around a principle, or prepare
a procedure that keeps uncertain provisions from being silently treated as
settled.

Choose what the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front's{!</span>!}
parliamentary team will make ready first.

[? if oil_project = "Term-by-term comparison": Earlier work has given deputies
a provision-by-provision comparison of the supplemental agreement. ?]
[? if oil_project = "Cross-faction majority": Earlier work has concentrated on
the language that potential supporters from several tendencies can defend. ?]
[? if oil_project = "Procedural commission record": Earlier work has separated
documented provisions from disputed and unknown ones. ?]
[? if oil_project = "Makki committee examination":
{!<span class="term term-makki">!}Makki{!</span>!} has already pressed the
committee to examine the agreement against the demand for national control. ?]
[? if oil_project_setbacks > 0: Institutional pressure has already interrupted
the parliamentary work behind that record. ?]

- @oil_terms
- @oil_coalition
- @oil_record
- @return_card

@oil_terms
title: Compare proposal terms one by one
subtitle: Ownership, operations, marketing, and compensation are separated before the <span class="term term-national-front">Front</span> names its floor
on-arrival: {!
var mature = Q.oil_project_stage >= 2;
Q.oil_project = "Term-by-term comparison";
Q.oil_project_stage += 1;
Q.parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += mature ? 1 : 5;
Q.oil_coalition_support += mature ? 1 : 2;
Q.oil_terms_position = "Term-by-term national control";
!}

The team creates a term-by-term comparison and leaves disputed provisions
blank rather than assigning them a neutral value. Deputies can now see whether
a proposal recognizes Iranian ownership, transfers operational authority, or
merely changes payment language.

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}'s minimum
becomes more precise and its committee case more
defensible. A careful table does not by itself create the votes to adopt it.
[? if oil_project_stage > 2: The principal provisions have already been
separated and compared. Another examination can correct the argument, but
adds less than persuading deputies who have not yet accepted its conclusion. ?]

- @post_event: Continue

@oil_coalition
title: Build a cross-faction committee majority
subtitle: Support is assembled around the parts of national control each faction can defend
on-arrival: {!
var repeated = Q.oil_project === "Cross-faction majority";
var mature = Q.oil_project_stage >= 2;
Q.oil_project = "Cross-faction majority";
Q.oil_project_stage += 1;
Q.oil_coalition_support += mature ? 2 : 6;
Q.independent_nationalists_relation += mature ? 0 : 2;
Q.religious_network_relation += mature ? 0 : 2;
if (repeated && mature) Q.parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy -= 1;
!}

{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} deputies meet
prospective supporters separately, asking what language
they can defend and where they will refuse compromise. {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Independent
nationalists{!</span>!} and {!<span class="term term-religious">!}religious-network{!</span>!} allies see their priorities reflected in
the effort.

The committee majority becomes more plausible. Some technical terms remain
unresolved because the work has concentrated on common ground.
[? if oil_project_stage > 2: Most readily persuadable deputies have already
been approached. Further bargaining secures a few wavering commitments, but
risks making the committee appear more concerned with votes than with the
substance of the agreement. ?]

- @post_event: Continue

@oil_record
title: Mark what the agreement does—and does not—settle
subtitle: Deputies and editors receive the same careful account of documented, disputed, and unknown provisions
on-arrival: {!
var mature = Q.oil_project_stage >= 2;
Q.oil_project = "Procedural commission record";
Q.oil_project_stage += 1;
Q.parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += mature ? 2 : 5;
Q.press_capacity += mature ? 1 : 3;
!}

The parliamentary team labels every provision as documented, disputed, or
still unknown and attaches the strongest available record. Friendly editors
receive the same distinctions so that uncertainty is not reported as a
compromise.

Committee work becomes more transparent and the public explanation improves.
The record deliberately stops short of selecting the final acceptable terms.
[? if oil_project_stage > 2: The remaining uncertainty cannot be eliminated by
reformatting the same evidence. Another review preserves the distinction
between fact and dispute, but wins less new confidence than the first. ?]

- @post_event: Continue
